# Names only — never real values. This is the one .env* file .gitignore does not
# ignore, and core/security/nfr2-guard.test.ts reads it on every test run.
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. The application builds and tests without these;
# it cannot sign anyone in or store a document without them.

# Postgres (Railway). Two roles, per AD-4: the writer is used by ingestion and
# sign-in; the reader is SELECT-only and is the only role catalog queries use.
WATCHDOG_WRITER_DATABASE_URL=
WATCHDOG_READER_DATABASE_URL=

# Auth.js session signing key. Generate with: npx auth secret
AUTH_SECRET=

# S3-compatible object storage for document bytes (AD-16).
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET=

# Optional, and only read by scripts/verify-storage.mjs. Name a bucket that
# exists in this account but sits outside the token's scope, and the script will
# assert the token is genuinely refused (403) rather than merely unable to find
# it (404). Unset, that check reports SKIP — it cannot tell a scoped token from
# an unscoped one pointed at a bucket that does not exist.
R2_OUT_OF_SCOPE_BUCKET=

# Document extraction (AD-9, AD-10). The extraction credential is a *different*
# credential from the reasoning model's, in a different deploy unit, and that
# separation is the boundary itself rather than a convention — see
# core/security/dual-llm-boundary.ts.
#
# It is no longer a *vendor* boundary: reasoning moved to `gemini-3.6-flash` on
# 2026-08-10, so both sides are Google. Two keys, not one, is therefore the whole
# of the credential layer. The reasoning key (`REASONING_API_KEY`) lives in the
# agent service's environment and is deliberately absent from this file — story
# 3.4 adds it, and nothing reads it before then.
#
# The model is selected by variable so changing it is configuration, not a code
# edit. Names only here; values live in .env.local, which is never committed.
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_OCR_MODEL=

# The agent service's identity (AD-15). The Python reasoning service presents
# this as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` to /tools/v1/*; the gateway accepts no
# other caller.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE DEPLOYING. AD-15 gives the tool endpoints two protections --
# a private network and this token -- and the Railway private network does not
# exist yet (epics.md, 2026-08-07). Until it does, this value is the ONLY thing
# between the public internet and the association's records.
#
# Unset or blank means the endpoint refuses every caller, deliberately: an
# absent secret is when the endpoint is most exposed and least watched, so it
# fails closed rather than treating "nothing configured" as "nothing to check".
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
AGENT_SERVICE_TOKEN=

# The reasoning model (AD-11, AD-10). Read by the *agent service*, never by the
# gateway, and deliberately not named GEMINI_API_KEY or GOOGLE_API_KEY.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE DEPLOYING. Extraction and reasoning are both Google since
# 2026-08-10, so AD-10's vendor clause is withdrawn and the separate credential
# is the whole of what is left of that boundary. CrewAI picks a key up from the
# environment if it is not handed one, and prefers GOOGLE_API_KEY over
# GEMINI_API_KEY -- so setting either of those in the agent's environment
# silently hands the reasoning model the extraction credential, and everything
# keeps working. agent/tests/test_no_data_credentials.py fails if the service
# ever reads them.
#
# REASONING_MODEL is optional and defaults to the spine's binding. The id is
# seed, not invariant (AD-11); the capability bar is what binds.
REASONING_API_KEY=
REASONING_MODEL=

# The Node->agent chat turn (AD-17). The gateway calls the agent service here;
# the agent calls back through /tools/v1/* under AGENT_SERVICE_TOKEN above.
#
# TWO TOKENS, DELIBERATELY. AD-17: "one token reused in both directions means
# either runtime's compromise grants the other's identity." Do not set these to
# the same value as AGENT_SERVICE_TOKEN.
#
# Both runtimes read GATEWAY_SERVICE_TOKEN -- the gateway to present it, the
# agent to check it. Only the gateway reads AGENT_BASE_URL.
#
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
GATEWAY_SERVICE_TOKEN=
AGENT_BASE_URL=

# The actor assertion signing key (AD-18). **A THIRD credential, and the one
# that is easiest to mistake for a fourth copy of the other two.**
#
# The two tokens above authenticate *runtimes*: they establish that a caller is
# the agent service, or is the gateway. Neither carries a subject, so neither
# says which board member a turn is for -- and since story 5.1b that decides
# which association's records come back.
#
# ONLY THE GATEWAY READS THIS. Node mints an assertion on the way out and
# verifies it on the way back in; the agent service relays it and must never be
# given the key. If this value appears in the agent's environment, the property
# AD-18 rests on is gone -- the relay could mint an assertion for anyone.
#
# Unset refuses every turn rather than sending one the gateway will reject.
#
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
ACTOR_ASSERTION_KEY=

# Where the *agent* finds the gateway, for the callback half of the same wire.
#
# TWO URLs, DELIBERATELY, and this one is easy to miss because the traffic runs
# the other way: AGENT_BASE_URL above is how Node reaches the agent's /chat/v1/*,
# and this is how the agent reaches Node's /tools/v1/* to get the rows an answer
# is built from. AD-3 is why it needs one at all -- the agent holds no data
# credential and obtains every fact by calling here.
#
# Read only by the agent service. `https` is required and enforced, not
# preferred: the service token travels on it.
#
# Absent, the agent starts fine and fails on the first question that needs
# records, because it is read at call time -- a board member sees "cannot access
# the records at this time", which is true and says nothing about the cause. It
# was missing from this file until the first deploy hit exactly that.
GATEWAY_BASE_URL=

# Outbound mail (FR-8, AD-8). The alert email is the only thing this system
# sends, and the only thing it sends on its own initiative.
#
# The provider is CONFIGURATION, NOT CODE. `MAIL_API_URL` is the full endpoint,
# read at call time, so swapping providers is a value here rather than a code
# change. It must be `https:` -- an `http:` value is refused, because the key
# below travels to whatever this names in an `Authorization` header, the same
# rule AGENT_BASE_URL carries and for the same reason. The body posted is `{from, to, subject, text}` as JSON with the key as
# `Authorization: Bearer` -- the shape Resend and Postmark both accept. SMTP was
# the alternative and would have meant a dependency, a connection pool and TLS
# configuration; story 4.7 took the same tie the same way when it chose CSV over
# a PDF library.
#
# Unset means alerting is off entirely: no finding is read, nothing is claimed,
# and no delivery record of any kind is written. The named error is logged once
# per ingestion, so a mailer that is silently absent is distinguishable from one
# that had nothing to send. Nothing crashes and no upload fails.
#
# A reason IS recorded against a finding once it has been claimed and processing
# it fails -- a refused send, but also anything else that goes wrong between the
# claim and the record. That is a different state from being unconfigured, and
# it is the one a later run retries.
#
# The application builds and tests without any of these; `next build` must not
# require them.
#
# The message is plain text and has no HTML part. AD-8: extracted values are
# data, never instructions, and a vendor name lifted off a scanned invoice
# cannot become markup that does not exist.
MAIL_API_URL=
MAIL_API_KEY=
MAIL_FROM=

# Where this application is, as a board member's browser would reach it.
#
# The alert email deep-links to a finding, and a relative path is meaningless in
# an inbox. Absolute, with a scheme: `https://watchdog.example.com`. A trailing
# slash is fine and is removed.
#
# `http:` is accepted here, unlike AGENT_BASE_URL above -- that one carries a
# service token and its scheme is a credential boundary, while this one is an
# address a director follows. A pilot behind a local relay is an ordinary state.
WATCHDOG_BASE_URL=
